WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.706
Welcome to Module Four: Cluster Test and Verification.

2
00:00:04.036 --> 00:00:06.386
It's where you prove a new cluster is healthy

3
00:00:06.436 --> 00:00:08.466
before anyone trusts it with real work.

4
00:00:09.266 --> 00:00:15.509
Picture a brand-new 256-GPU cluster handed straight to researchers, with no burn-in.

5
00:00:15.839 --> 00:00:19.672
Their first big training run crawls, far slower than the specs promised.

6
00:00:20.472 --> 00:00:24.433
The culprit: one node's all-reduce bandwidth is half that of its peers.

7
00:00:24.763 --> 00:00:29.278
A swapped rail cable and a transceiver on old firmware drag every step down.

8
00:00:30.078 --> 00:00:34.913
Then, two hours into a long run, one GPU starts throwing errors under load.

9
00:00:35.243 --> 00:00:39.631
The job crashes, and a week of cluster time disappears into finger-pointing.

10
00:00:40.431 --> 00:00:43.367
Every one of these faults was findable before handover.

11
00:00:43.697 --> 00:00:46.753
Systematic testing, from a single node up to the whole fabric,

12
00:00:46.803 --> 00:00:48.106
would have caught them all.

13
00:00:48.906 --> 00:00:55.149
Lesson one starts on a single node, with DCGM diagnostics run through dcgmi.

14
00:00:55.479 --> 00:00:59.735
The longer diagnostic levels stress every GPU and flag memory,

15
00:00:59.785 --> 00:01:01.680
power, and thermal problems.

16
00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:08.019
Then comes HPL, the dense linear-algebra benchmark behind the TOP500 list.

17
00:01:08.349 --> 00:01:10.447
A short run checks performance,

18
00:01:10.497 --> 00:01:14.465
and hours of HPL burn-in expose GPUs that fail under heat.

19
00:01:15.265 --> 00:01:19.737
Lesson two turns to NCCL, the library that moves data between GPUs.

20
00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:24.391
Its all-reduce tests measure NVLink Switch bandwidth inside each node.

21
00:01:25.191 --> 00:01:28.895
Across nodes, the same tests measure east-west fabric bandwidth.

22
00:01:29.225 --> 00:01:33.591
A slow node stands out immediately, and long runs become a fabric burn-in.

23
00:01:34.391 --> 00:01:37.413
Lesson three validates cabling and signal quality.

24
00:01:37.743 --> 00:01:40.811
You'll confirm every cable lands in the right port,

25
00:01:40.861 --> 00:01:44.051
then read link errors and the bit error rate, or BER.

26
00:01:44.851 --> 00:01:48.171
Lesson four confirms firmware and software versions everywhere.

27
00:01:48.501 --> 00:01:53.955
Switches, BlueField-3 DPUs, and even transceivers must match the approved baseline.

28
00:01:54.755 --> 00:01:56.545
Lesson five brings in ClusterKit,

29
00:01:56.595 --> 00:02:00.615
which runs a battery of health and performance checks across the cluster.

30
00:02:00.945 --> 00:02:04.777
It compares every node against its peers, so outliers can't hide.

31
00:02:05.577 --> 00:02:09.687
A NeMo burn-in then trains a real model across the cluster for hours.

32
00:02:10.017 --> 00:02:13.061
It's the closest thing to the researchers' actual workload.

33
00:02:13.861 --> 00:02:17.629
Storage gets tested too, with read and write throughput benchmarks.

34
00:02:17.959 --> 00:02:22.325
Slow storage starves GPUs just as surely as a bad cable does.

35
00:02:23.125 --> 00:02:26.318
Run it all, and the cluster reaches researchers at full speed.

36
00:02:26.648 --> 00:02:30.246
Every result is saved as a baseline for troubleshooting later.

37
00:02:31.046 --> 00:02:34.900
This is the largest domain: thirty-three percent, a third of the exam.

38
00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:39.084
Lesson one starts on a single node, with stress tests and HPL.
